steps.body:
Level-order traversal visits the tree row by row, top to bottom and left to right. That is the level-by-level BFS from the Breadth-First Search lesson, with a `Queue<TreeNode>` instead of grid cells:

```java
List<List<Integer>> levels = new ArrayList<>();
Queue<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
queue.offer(root);
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int size = queue.size();
    List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        TreeNode node = queue.poll();
        level.add(node.val);
        if (node.left != null) {
            queue.offer(node.left);
        }
        if (node.right != null) {
            queue.offer(node.right);
        }
    }
    levels.add(level);
}
```

Two details differ from a grid:

- There is no visited check. Every node has exactly one parent, so it is offered only once.
- Offer a child only if it is not `null`. `ArrayDeque` rejects `null` and throws `NullPointerException`.

`int size = queue.size();` is saved before the inner loop, because the queue grows as children are added.

Guard the start too: offer `root` only when it is not `null`. Each level becomes its own `List<Integer>` inside `levels`.

