steps.body:
A binary search tree (BST) keeps one rule at every node: everything in the left subtree is smaller and everything in the right subtree is larger. Insert follows the rule down to an empty spot:

```java
static TreeNode insert(TreeNode node, int val) {
    if (node == null) {
        return new TreeNode(val);
    }
    if (val < node.val) {
        node.left = insert(node.left, val);
    } else if (val > node.val) {
        node.right = insert(node.right, val);
    }
    return node;
}
```

Always assign the result: `root = insert(root, x)`. That is how the first insert replaces a `null` root. An equal value is already in the tree, so this insert skips it, just as `TreeSet` ignores a duplicate.

The rule pays off everywhere:

- Search goes left or right at each node, so it takes at most one step per level, O(height), instead of visiting every node.
- An inorder traversal of a BST comes out sorted.
- The min is the leftmost node and the max is the rightmost: keep following `left` (or `right`) until it is `null`.
- To find the largest value below a target, walk down and remember the best candidate. When a node is smaller than the target, record it and go right to look for a closer one. Otherwise go left.

Gotcha: a plain BST never rebalances. Sorted inserts build a one-sided chain of height n. `TreeMap` and `TreeSet` are balanced BSTs, so their `floorKey` and `ceiling` stay fast.

example.code:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        TreeNode root = null;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            root = insert(root, sc.nextInt());
        }
        int target = sc.nextInt();
        System.out.println("Contains " + target + ": " + contains(root, target));
        TreeNode low = root;
        while (low.left != null) {
            low = low.left;
        }
        TreeNode high = root;
        while (high.right != null) {
            high = high.right;
        }
        System.out.println("Min: " + low.val);
        System.out.println("Max: " + high.val);
        Integer below = largestBelow(root, target);
        if (below == null) {
            System.out.println("Largest below " + target + ": none");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Largest below " + target + ": " + below);
            System.out.println("Contains " + below + ": " + contains(root, below));
        }
    }

    static TreeNode insert(TreeNode node, int val) {
        if (node == null) {
            return new TreeNode(val);
        }
        if (val < node.val) {
            node.left = insert(node.left, val);
        } else if (val > node.val) {
            node.right = insert(node.right, val);
        }
        return node;
    }

    static boolean contains(TreeNode node, int val) {
        while (node != null && node.val != val) {
            node = val < node.val ? node.left : node.right;
        }
        return node != null;
    }

    static Integer largestBelow(TreeNode node, int target) {
        Integer best = null;
        while (node != null) {
            if (node.val < target) {
                best = node.val;
                node = node.right;
            } else {
                node = node.left;
            }
        }
        return best;
    }
}

